 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "In this repo (Tessel, a Flask-style framework), `request`, `session` and `g` reach templates in two ways:\n\n- **`g` and `request`** are added per render by a default context processor, `_base_template_vars()` in `src/tessel/templating.py:21`. It returns the concrete `ctx.g` and, when a request is active, `ctx.request`. It is registered as the first entry in `template_ctx_injectors` in `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:182-184`.\n- **`session`** is not in that dict. The proxy is set as a Jinja global in `create_jinja_environment()` (`src/tessel/app.py:492-503`), along with `request` and `g` for imported templates. The proxy itself is defined in `src/tessel/globals.py:48-61`.\n\nTemplates are rendered through `render_template()` in `src/tessel/templating.py`, which calls `app.fill_template_scope()` (`src/tessel/app.py:588-616`). That runs the injectors and then re-applies the caller's own kwargs, so explicit `render_template` arguments win.\n\n**Adding your own variables:** use the decorator `@app.ctx_injector`, defined in `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:549-564`. The function returns a dict whose keys become template variables:\n\n```python\n@app.ctx_injector\ndef inject_user():\n    return {\"current_user\": ...}\n```\n\nFor a bundle, use `@bundle.app_ctx_injector` (`src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py:644`), which applies to every template rendered by the app rather than only that bundle's views. For a global function rather than a variable, `src/tessel/sansio/app.py:825` shows `jinja_env.globals` being populated.",
